# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
policy_module(ai_tools_buildexec, 0.1.0)

# ai_tools OPTIONAL GROUP: buildexec  [DEFAULT: DISABLED]
#
# Execute what a build produced inside a claimed project: a native host the session BUILT
# from the tree (a .NET apphost, a test host, a ReadyToRun image under `dotnet exec`), which
# needs execute on a project file, and the base grants that nowhere. The grant is on
# ai_tools_project_build_t, the type the base declares for build output without naming a
# directory for it. Which directories carry it is not this group's to say either: each toolchain integration
# ships a LAYOUT module (ai_tools_dotnet.te for .NET) that maps its output directories onto the
# type at creation, and declares the same names in its manifest for relabel time. This module
# is one rule, so an administrator reads it as the grant it is, whatever toolchain is in use.
#
# SENSITIVE. This IS a boundary: on-disk code the sandbox can write, executed as a new process
# image. It does not grant a new PRIVILEGE (execmem already concedes in-process native code,
# and execute_no_trans keeps the child in ai_tools_t with no entrypoint to a more privileged
# domain), but it is the reason the module is off by default. The scoping to build output is a
# CONVENIENCE, not a security control: the type follows the directory name, so a script the
# session writes under a build directory, and a source directory that happens to share such a
# name, carry it too. What it changes is that enabling the group does not make git run a
# project's hooks. An IL-only assembly run through a host binary (`dotnet exec App.dll`)
# does not execute any project file and does not need this group.

require {
    type ai_tools_t;
    type ai_tools_project_build_t;
}

# Execute a native host or R2R-mapped assembly from the build output. execute_no_trans
# keeps it in ai_tools_t (no entrypoint elsewhere); execmod covers an R2R image relocated
# in place; map is the executable file mapping (already base-granted on this writable
# type, listed for completeness). ai_tools_project_t is deliberately absent: a git hook,
# `./configure`, or a Makefile recipe's script stays non-executable with this group loaded.
allow ai_tools_t ai_tools_project_build_t:file { map execute execute_no_trans execmod };
